WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.470
Here's the deal, I'm about to drop some mad
Things You Missed from the new movie IT, but

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:13.750
before I do, if you are interested in winning
some free CZsWorld merch, in my free merch

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.330
giveaway, watch this entire video and you'll
find out how.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:22.119
Bill has some posters in his room, and I was
able to identify a couple of them as Gremlins

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:23.530
and Beetlejuice.

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.810
Why is this significant?

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.040
Well, I think these posters both have double-meanings
behind them.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.540
The first, is a foreshadowing of Bill's future.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.380
You'll find that after facing their fears
in this movie, that none of losers are losers

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:36.380
anymore.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.850
They all go on to have very successful careers,
perhaps the most successful of all of them

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.910
though, is Bill Denborough, who grows up to
be a horror writer.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.400
Like me.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.129
But without the stupid goatee.

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:52.269
But it's cool to see that Bill does have an
interest in horror as a kid that leads into

00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:54.239
his occupation as an adult.

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:58.409
On a side note, pretty much every Stephen
King novel has a protagonist who is a writer,

00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:01.629
a method he uses to project himself into his
work.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:05.700
Muschietti and his art department could have
chosen any two horror movie posters to hang

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.730
on Bill's wall though, so why did he choose
these?

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.230
For one, Bill's still a kid, and these are
both more kid friendly horror movies that

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.090
came out in the 1980s.

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.920
But I think there's also a reason that these
were chosen as opposed to say...

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:19.920
Ghostbusters.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.550
First let's look at Gremlins.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:26.720
It's a movie about these little things that
change into other more scary looking things.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.240
And in this movie, IT's shapeshifting abilities
are on full display, one of which being the

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.990
transformation that IT does with it's teeth
when feeding off the fear of the children.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.790
It seemed like it took some big inspiration
from Barbara's transformation in Beetlejuice,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:46.030
how the face kind of peels back to reveal
these rows and rows of razor sharp teeth.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:51.060
Especially that scene where Pennywise is about
to feed on Stan, I was getting major vibes

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.930
from the Tim Burton classic.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:56.990
This next one is really only a Thing You Missed
if you haven't read the book, or at least

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.280
if it's been a while.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.040
During the beginning of the film, you may
have heard Bill muttering the following phrase,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.320
"He thrusts his fists against the posts and
still insists he sees the ghosts."

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.619
It comes back again at the end of the film
I believe.

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:14.129
What this is, is a coping mechanism that Bill
uses to help with his stutter, and it's something

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:18.340
that helps him overcome Pennywise in the book,
because his stutter is directly linked to

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.860
his fear of IT.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.440
It's not that bad at first, but it gets worse
when Georgie is killed and whenever his fear

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.910
of IT increases, the stutter gets worse.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.719
Remember when he has the courage to go into
the 29 Neibolt House after they discover that

00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:34.579
it's the old well house and it's connected
to the sewer?

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:38.459
He gives his friends a speech, and Richie
even comments on him not having stuttered

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:39.819
through any of it.

00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:43.730
That's his courage that allows him to stand
up like that, so when you hear him say that

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.799
phrase, "he thrusts his fists against the
posts and still insists he sees the ghosts,"

00:02:47.799 --> 00:02:52.870
without stuttering, you know that he has grown
in the face of fear in order to do so, and

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.370
that's what weakens IT.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.810
I would expect to hear more of that phrase
when his childhood stutter comes back in Chapter

00:02:58.810 --> 00:02:59.840
2.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.660
There were a lot of little nods to events
and characters from the book that were touched

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.170
on in the movie, but didn't need to be fully
expanded.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.790
Some of these things had their own chapter
in the book, but I think the film did a nice

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.370
job of only including what was necessary,
as not to overwhelm the viewer.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.599
Here are a few of those references.

00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:22.319
Ben researches The Ironworks Explosion, a
catastrophe caused by IT in the early 1900s.

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:26.590
He also looks up the Black Spot Fire, which
was a nightclub in Derry, during...

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:32.640
I was to say the 1930s, that was a hangout
for a lot of African-American men at the time.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.169
It was burned down by a racist faction, with
only only a few survivors, one of which being

00:03:37.169 --> 00:03:40.980
Mike Hanlon's dad, who passes the story onto
his son.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.909
The relevancy of that in this movie is debatable,
because Mike's parents actually do die in

00:03:45.909 --> 00:03:51.769
a fire prior to the events of 1989, and the
whole racism subplot is not really so applicable

00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:53.480
with this taking place in the 80s.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.269
One of the reasons Henry Bowers hates Mike
so much, is that Henry's father held a great

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:02.950
deal of racism towards Mike's father and that
racism carried down into Henry.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.329
It's possible that some of that hatred is
present in the movie, because we do see Henry

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:12.099
single out Mike a couple of times, the first
being just before the rock fight, and the

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:16.389
second being when Henry catches up with Mike
as the Losers are descending into the well

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:17.910
and nearly kills him.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.160
I didn't notice any specifically racist remarks
though.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.960
Let me know if you felt any of that tension
in the comments.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.199
Back to some of the book references.

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:31.169
Many of the victims were only called out by
name, or at least had their names show up

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:32.699
on missing posters.

00:04:32.699 --> 00:04:37.430
I noticed that a couple of these were actual
characters from the book, like Betty Ripson

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.780
and Edward Corcoran.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.370
I tried to read all of the missing posters
to make connections, but I may have missed

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:48.090
some of them given that I have no way of pausing
this movie in the theaters, so again, chime

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:52.060
in in the comments and let me know what you
saw, I'm genuinely interested.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:57.370
One location that is brought up, but I don't
think we actually seen on screen is The Standpipe,

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:01.050
which is kind of a landmark in Derry which
is connected to the stream in the Barrens

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.690
and the sewer system.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:07.030
Only reason I bring it up, is that it's probably
more than a throwaway reference, as it's something

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.729
that's likely to come into play in Chapter
2.

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:11.479
That's all I'll say for now.

00:05:11.479 --> 00:05:16.300
At one point Pennywise encounters Richie and
uses the line, "beep, beep Richie!" which

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.150
is a phrase that the Lucky 7 always uses in
the book whenever Richie won't shut up.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:25.470
It doesn't come up a whole lot in the movie,
but Richie does still run his mouth a lot.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.909
The reason I wanted to bring it up, was that
I feel it kind of takes on a new meaning here,

00:05:29.909 --> 00:05:32.540
with how much of a Pottymouth Richie is in
this movie.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.629
The beep beep could represent the redacted
expletives.

00:05:36.629 --> 00:05:41.100
Speaking of Richie, did anyone else catch
that Nightmare on Elm Street was playing on

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.360
Derry's movie theater marquee?

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.150
That movie came out in 84, I don't know if
it ever came back out into cinemas in '89,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.680
but you would think there would be a good
reason for putting that up there in the wrong

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.680
year.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.770
I thought for sure Richie's fear was going
to be Freddy Krueger and that IT was going

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:01.659
to take the form of Freddy, but it turns out
he's just afraid of clowns.

00:06:01.659 --> 00:06:03.260
One other note on Richie.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.720
At the school, when Richie walks by the gang
of bullies, Patrick Hockstetter, the one who

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:13.040
later goes after them with a makeshift flamethrower,
gives him this creepy smile and licks his

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.639
lips.

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:17.889
One thing this movie was very good at is telling
a lot of backstory visually.

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:24.470
Patrick is one messed up kid who gets off
on hurting others and has weird violent homosexual

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:25.470
tendencies.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:30.040
At one point in the book he actually molests
Henry Bowers, which is why I think he's actually

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.970
the scariest human character.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.990
He doesn't get that far in the movie, but
that little lick of the lips at a boy who

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:41.629
is substantially younger than him tells us
that something is not right about this character.

00:06:41.629 --> 00:06:45.120
There were a couple of things that were different
from the book, but I think actually added

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.540
to some of the character arcs in the movie.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:52.530
For example, while Stan is Jewish in both
the novel and motion picture, his religion

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.800
plays a much bigger part now.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.789
Stan is studying for his bar-mitzvah, and
his father is the Rabbi of the temple, but

00:06:58.789 --> 00:07:03.430
we're given the idea that Stan isn't really
a big believer because he hasn't been studying

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:04.639
his Torah portion.

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:08.271
In fact, the blessing that he's having trouble
with is actually just the prayer you have

00:07:08.271 --> 00:07:12.710
to recite before opening the Torah, so it's
like the easiest one because it always comes

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:13.710
up.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:14.849
But he can't even get through that.

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:19.340
The reason I think this is kind of a brilliant
change from the book, is that the whole basis

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.879
of Stan's character is that he's the last
one to truly believe in IT.

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:27.620
He even says that the things that they are
all seeing aren't real.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:31.050
Until the Neibolt House encounter.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.300
And it hits him the hardest.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.940
Once he believes in IT, he's probably the
most scared of IT.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:41.039
And right after that happens, we're led into
the montage of the middle of that summer where

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:43.159
he actually has his bar-mitzvah.

00:07:43.159 --> 00:07:47.300
His belief in IT, mirrors his faith in his
religion.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.960
In my Things You Missed videos on the trailers
for this movie, I talked about the meaning

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.419
behind the graphics on some of the kids T-shirts
and how Metal could now play the part that

00:07:55.419 --> 00:07:57.340
Rock &amp; Roll played in the original.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:01.180
Upon seeing the movie, I have another example
of that.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:05.680
During the rock fight, Belch wears a t-shirt
repping a demo CD that Metallica put out in

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.180
the 80s called Metal Up Your Ass.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.919
The graphic features a hand holding a sword
coming out of the toilet.

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:17.639
Perfect choice for a movie about a dangerous
clown creature who also comes out of the sewers.

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:19.440
Let's talk more about that clown.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.870
At the end of the basement scene, Pennywise's
eyes roll back in his head before he sinks

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.550
back into the water.

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:30.210
Remember how I pointed out that in the teaser
Pennywise's eyes didn't reflect in the water?

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:34.150
And then in the Official Trailer, they fixed
it and some of us were kind of disappointed

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:37.029
because the eyeless void looked kind of cool?

00:08:37.029 --> 00:08:39.680
Maybe this was kind of a redemption.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.139
Also Bev's eyes do that same thing at the
end, when Pennywise kidnaps her and she is

00:08:44.139 --> 00:08:48.459
floating above the ground, her eye sockets
are just white, so that may have been saying

00:08:48.459 --> 00:08:50.590
that Pennywise was controlling her.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:54.620
I have more on that floating thing, so I'll
get back to that.

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:57.870
I noticed a couple things when Bill goes into
Georgie's room.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:02.740
In a previous video, I pointed out the circus
animals on the wallpaper behind Bill.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:06.621
As it turns out, that is part of Georgie's
room, but it's not just the wallpaper, the

00:09:06.621 --> 00:09:09.300
whole thing is themed like a circus.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:13.300
That's kind of creepy when you take into account
the fact that IT is using Georgie's image

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.649
as a puppet to trick Bill throughout the entire
thing.

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:21.220
You almost wonder if Pennywise attacked Georgie
first for a reason.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:25.560
The other thing I noticed was the LEGO turtle
that Bill finds in his brother's old room,

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.320
which was one of a few references to a celestial
being known as the Turtle who is an opposing

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.709
force against IT.

00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:35.180
The Turtle is kind of slyly written into the
book.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:39.040
Characters will find themselves thinking about
The Turtle without really knowing why.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.889
In the movie, one of the characters brings
up the fact that they think see a turtle during

00:09:42.889 --> 00:09:44.510
the swimming scene.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.610
And I'm not entirely sure because, again,
I can't pause yet, but I'm pretty sure I saw

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:51.700
a turtle sticker on one of Beverly's books.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:55.290
These references are nothing more than Easter
Eggs though, because the primary scene in

00:09:55.290 --> 00:10:00.250
which The Turtle gives advice to Bill, known
as the Ritual of Chud, is not a scene in this

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:01.250
movie.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:05.691
The Turtle's rival, who the kids refer to
as IT, but whose true form is known as The

00:10:05.691 --> 00:10:08.490
Deadlights, does make a brief appearance.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:12.690
When Pennywise tries to eat Beverly, and it's
face peels back, she looks down it's throat

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.820
and sees three bright lights.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.560
This is somewhat different than the form that
we're used to seeing The Deadlights in.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:23.750
They appear to be more of a force within Pennywise
as opposed to their own powerful entity.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:28.070
Nobody in the movie ever uses the word "deadlights",
so they make their way onto the silver screen

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:29.800
in a much more subtle way.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.480
This next one I didn't notice until the last
scene of the movie.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.339
In a previous Things You Missed I pointed
out that Beverly wears a key around her neck

00:10:37.339 --> 00:10:39.630
and speculated what it might be for.

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:45.839
Many, and I mean a lot, like 666 billion of
you pointed out that they key is uncut so

00:10:45.839 --> 00:10:48.120
it couldn't actually be used for unlocking
anything.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.601
After seeing the movie, I still have no idea
what the significance of the key is, but I

00:10:52.601 --> 00:10:57.690
noticed another piece of jewelry that Bev
has: it's a ring with an anchor on it.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:59.110
What do anchors do?

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:00.170
They sink.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:03.990
And after countless cases of "you'll float
too, you'll float to, you'll float too, you'll

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:09.510
float too," the group eventually finds that
Beverly "floats" as well after being abducted

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:10.510
by Pennywise.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:15.140
But because the love and dedication of her
friends, they're able to find her, and pull

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:17.380
her back down and out of harm's way.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:22.389
In a world where all the victims float, she's
the one who sinks.

00:11:22.389 --> 00:11:23.639
She's the anchor.

00:11:23.639 --> 00:11:27.850
In that final scene, after the promise, Bev
tells what she remembers from when she was

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:32.600
afloat and says she had a vision of them when
they were all much older, in their 30s.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:34.690
This is a foreshadowing of the sequel.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:35.690
Chapter 2.

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:41.080
When, after a 27 year rest, Pennywise reemerges
and the losers are called back to Derry to

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.850
fulfill their promise and vanquish IT.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:47.980
And that ties into the last thing you missed,
which is really only a thing you would miss

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:52.170
if you didn't stay for the credits, because
Pennywise's laughter can be heard at the end

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.760
of them, signalling that this fight isn't
over yet.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:12:00.300
But the fact that there was a title that said
Chapter 1 should have given it away already,

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.779
if you didn't catch on from that I don't know
what's wrong with you.

00:12:02.779 --> 00:12:05.449
One thing I liked about this movie was the
look of it.

00:12:05.449 --> 00:12:11.180
It looks very sharp, and now you too can look
